<article class="env-lemma" id="0A4J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0A4J">Lemma <span data-tag="0A4J">68.12.5</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Dimension as defined in Properties of Spaces, Section <a href="/tag/04N3" data-tag="04N3">66.9</a> behaves well on decent algebraic spaces $X$ over $S$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $x \in |X|$, then $\dim _ x(|X|) = \dim _ x(X)$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\dim (|X|) = \dim (X)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proof of (1). Choose a scheme $U$ with a point $u \in U$ and an étale morphism $h : U \to X$ mapping $u$ to $x$. By definition the dimension of $X$ at $x$ is $\dim _ u(|U|)$. Thus we may pick $U$ such that $\dim _ x(X) = \dim (|U|)$. Let $d$ be an integer. If $\dim (U) \geq d$, then there exists a sequence of nontrivial specializations $u_ d \leadsto \ldots \leadsto u_0$ in $U$. Taking the image we find a corresponding sequence $h(u_ d) \leadsto \ldots \leadsto h(u_0)$ each of which is nontrivial by Lemma <a href="/tag/03K5" data-tag="03K5">68.12.1</a>. Hence we see that the image of $|U|$ in $|X|$ has dimension at least $d$. Conversely, suppose that $x_ d \leadsto \ldots \leadsto x_0$ is a sequence of specializations in $|X|$ with $x_0$ in the image of $|U| \to |X|$. Then we can lift this to a sequence of specializations in $U$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/03IL" data-tag="03IL">68.12.2</a>. 
    <p>
      Part (2) is an immediate consequence of part (1), Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B7I" data-tag="0B7I">5.10.2</a>, and Properties of Spaces, Section <a href="/tag/04N3" data-tag="04N3">66.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>